TrueSpend — Public Accounts, Security, Onboarding & Experience Tiers
Document type: Product and technical specification
Status: Draft for product review
Prepared: 15 November 2026
Target branch: feature/core-updates-audit
Scope: Public registration, custom authentication hardening, private user data isolation, Basic and Advanced experiences, onboarding, profile/security controls, and public-launch readiness.

1. Executive summary
TrueSpend is moving from a developer-operated personal finance application to a product that can be safely shared with friends and eventually opened to the public.

This update establishes the foundations required for that transition:

Anyone can create an account through public registration. An invitation code is optional.
Every account owns a completely separate private financial dataset. There are no shared workspaces, shared ledgers, or shared accounts.
The existing custom authentication system is retained and hardened. Firebase Authentication is not part of this design.
Every user chooses one of two free experiences: Basic or Advanced. Neither tier is paid, and payment processing is explicitly out of scope.
Basic remains a useful personal-finance product, not an empty demo. It removes selected advanced capabilities and simplifies others.
Advanced exposes the full TrueSpend toolset.
The user may change experience tier later without losing or duplicating financial data.
Onboarding guides the user through account creation and optional financial setup, with templates and a persistent getting-started checklist.
Account security, data export, account deletion, session management, privacy information, and public-launch protections are part of the scope.
Tier selection, user role, and ownership of financial data are separate concepts.
The work must start with security and data isolation. Public registration must not be enabled until the backend can reliably guarantee that one user cannot access another user's information.

