5. Public registration
5.1 Registration experience
Public registration must be available from the unauthenticated landing/sign-in experience.

Required fields:

Email address.
Password.
Confirm password.
Display name.
Optional fields:

Username/handle, if retained as a public identity feature.
Invitation code.
Do not request salary, balances, emergency buffer, employer or debt details during account creation. Those belong in optional onboarding steps after the account is verified.

5.2 Registration rules
Normalize email addresses consistently (trim whitespace and lowercase).
Enforce a database-level unique email constraint.
If usernames are enabled, normalize them and enforce case-insensitive uniqueness.
Use a password policy designed for public accounts. Recommended minimum: 12 characters, allow long passphrases, reject common/compromised passwords where a suitable service/library is available, and do not impose arbitrary character-composition rules.
Validate all fields on both client and server.
Never accept role, workspaceMode, userId, permissions, or owner status from the registration payload.
New public users always receive the standard member role.
New accounts always receive an isolated private data scope.
New accounts select Basic or Advanced; never trust a tier from an unvalidated client field.
New accounts begin in pending_verification state.
Financial APIs remain unavailable until email verification is completed.
Registration must create the user and required account defaults transactionally.
Duplicate registration must not reveal whether an email is already registered through a different response path.
Public registration must be rate-limited and abuse-protected.
5.3 Optional invitations
Public registration remains open even when invitation campaigns exist.

An optional invitation code may be used for:

Recording who invited a user.
Tracking referral or campaign source.
Enabling a temporary onboarding experience or introductory content.
Limiting access to a private beta feature only if explicitly defined later.
An invitation code must never:

Be required for ordinary registration.
Create a shared workspace.
Attach a user to the inviter's ledger.
Grant access to the inviter's account or financial records.
Elevate the new user's role or administrative permissions.
Override the user's tier choice.
Suggested invitation model:

Opaque, high-entropy invitation token.
Store only a hash of the token.
Optional creator/inviter user ID.
Expiry timestamp.
Maximum uses and current use count, if campaign limits are required.
Revocation timestamp.
Optional campaign/referral metadata.
Record redemption against the newly created account.
Invalid optional codes should produce a clear, non-sensitive validation message. Registration without a code must always work.

6. Custom authentication architecture
6.1 Direction
Keep TrueSpend's custom authentication and PostgreSQL-backed user identity. Do not migrate authentication to Firebase.

The current JWT-based model can be retained as a foundation, but the seven-day bearer token in localStorage should not remain the long-term session design.

6.2 Password storage
Prefer Argon2id for password hashing if a maintained Node.js implementation is compatible with the deployment environment.
If adding Argon2id is not suitable, use Node's asynchronous crypto.scrypt with reviewed parameters.
Do not use synchronous hashing in request handlers.
Store algorithm and parameters with the hash so future upgrades are possible.
Rehash after successful login when parameters or algorithm are outdated.
Never store plaintext passwords, reversible passwords, or password hints.
Use timing-safe comparison through the chosen implementation.
Add tests for hash creation, verification, invalid passwords and legacy hash migration.
Existing scrypt$... hashes must remain verifiable during migration. Successful login can upgrade a legacy hash to the new format without forcing everyone to reset passwords.

6.3 Access and refresh sessions
Recommended web session model:

Short-lived access token, approximately 5–15 minutes.
Long-lived refresh session with rotation and revocation.
Store refresh-token hashes in the database, not raw refresh tokens.
Use secure, HttpOnly, Secure, appropriately SameSite cookies for browser refresh credentials.
Keep access credentials in memory where practical.
Protect cookie-authenticated state-changing requests against CSRF.
Validate the Origin/Referer against an explicit allowlist.
Rotate refresh tokens on use.
Detect refresh-token reuse and revoke the affected session family.
Provide logout-current-session and logout-all-sessions operations.
The Android Capacitor client needs a deliberate native session transport. Do not assume browser cookie behavior is identical inside a WebView. If a secure native token store is needed, evaluate a maintained Capacitor secure-storage option and keep tokens out of ordinary local storage/preferences.

6.4 JWT secret and key management
Remove super_secret_fallback_key.
Production startup must fail when required authentication secrets are absent or too weak.
Use high-entropy, environment-managed secrets.
Support secret rotation with a controlled key identifier/verification period if needed.
Never commit secrets to the repository.
Separate development and production secrets.
Do not log JWTs, refresh tokens, reset tokens or verification tokens.
6.5 Authentication abuse protection
Rate-limit registration, login, verification resend, password-reset request and password-reset submission.
Apply limits by IP and normalized account identifier, with careful handling of NAT/shared networks.
Use progressive delays or temporary throttling after repeated login failures.
Keep error messages generic where revealing account existence creates risk.
Add request validation and body size limits.
Add security headers and strict TLS requirements in production.
Use explicit CORS origin allowlists; never reflect every incoming origin when credentials are enabled.
6.6 Email verification
Send a verification email after registration.
Use a high-entropy, one-time verification token.
Store only a cryptographic hash of the token.
Expire verification tokens (recommended: 24 hours).
Mark the account verified only after successful token validation.
Make verification tokens single-use.
Provide resend verification with rate limiting and generic responses.
Do not permit access to financial APIs before verification.
Allow access to the limited verification/account-help screens.
Reissuing a verification token should invalidate or supersede earlier active tokens.
6.7 Forgot and reset password
Forgot password accepts an email address.
Always return a generic response such as: “If an account exists for that email, we'll send password-reset instructions.”
Generate a high-entropy, one-time reset token.
Store only its hash.
Use a short expiry (recommended: 30 minutes).
Enforce single use and revoke previous reset tokens when appropriate.
Never send the current password or reveal whether the account exists.
On successful reset, invalidate all existing sessions and refresh tokens.
Notify the account email that its password has changed.
Do not automatically sign the user in after a reset unless the security design explicitly supports that behavior.
6.8 Change password and email
Changing a password requires a valid current session and current-password confirmation, unless using an active recovery flow.
Changing an email requires re-authentication and verification of the new address.
Keep the old verified email until the new address has been verified.
Notify the old email address of the attempted change.
Revoke other sessions after sensitive account changes.
Record account-security events without logging secrets.
