6.10 Two-factor authentication
Include two-factor authentication in the security architecture, but it does not need to block the first registration release.

Recommended progression:

First release: secure passwords, verified email, sessions, recovery, rate limiting and account-security notifications.
Follow-up: optional authenticator-app TOTP with recovery codes.
Later: passkeys/WebAuthn where supported.
Do not implement SMS OTP as the default second factor.

7. Private-account and data-isolation architecture
7.1 Non-negotiable invariant
An authenticated user may access only records owned by that same user account.

There are no shared ledgers or shared financial workspaces in this product.

This invariant applies to:

Transactions and transaction splits.
Wallets and balances.
Budgets and budget allocations.
Debts, repayments and settlement events.
Goals and contribution history.
Payroll records.
Subscriptions and recurring commitments.
Financial contexts.
Journal entries and journal lines.
Statement import batches and rows.
Review inbox items.
Financial rules and execution logs.
FX records and financial history.
Investments, orders, dividends and portfolio snapshots.
External wealth records.
Notifications and push subscriptions.
AI conversation context and any user-specific memory.
Exports, backups and restore operations.
Any new user-owned financial table added in future.
7.2 Authentication identity versus data scope
Replace the existing shared/isolated req.dbUser resolution with an explicit authenticated account identity:

req.authUser.id identifies the signed-in account.
A single, well-defined req.userId or equivalent trusted context identifies the owner of the requested financial data.
For normal customer requests, this data-owner ID is always the authenticated account ID.
Do not derive data ownership from a URL parameter, query parameter, request body, custom header or client-provided userId.
Do not redirect public accounts to a developer/owner ledger.
Do not rely on hiding another user's records in the frontend.
The backend should pass the authenticated user ID explicitly into repository/service methods.

7.3 Repository and query requirements
Every user-owned query must include the authenticated user_id predicate.
Every update/delete must scope both the record ID and authenticated user_id.
Every nested resource lookup must verify that the parent belongs to the authenticated user.
Bulk operations must use the same tenant scope.
Exports and backups must be generated from the authenticated account's own data only.
Background jobs must identify which account they are processing and must not reuse an unrelated user's scope.
Avoid generic repository methods that accept an arbitrary user ID from client-controlled input.
Review SQL joins for missing user ownership predicates.
Review count/statistics endpoints; global totals must not leak private account activity.
Do not return password hashes, reset tokens, verification tokens, session hashes, integration secrets or internal role metadata from profile APIs.
7.4 Database-level defense in depth
Application query scoping is required but should not be the only protection.

Audit the schema and introduce ownership constraints where feasible:

Add composite unique keys such as (user_id, id) on parent records where needed.
Use composite foreign keys for tenant-owned relationships, e.g. a transaction's user_id plus wallet_id must refer to a wallet owned by the same user.
Validate ownership for destination wallets, linked payrolls, goals, contexts, journal records and investment relationships.
Add indexes that begin with user_id for common tenant-scoped reads.
Review ON DELETE behavior so deleting an account cannot delete another user's data.
Use PostgreSQL row-level security only as an additional layer if the connection-pool/session-context design is tested carefully. It must not replace application authorization.
7.5 Roles and administrative separation
Public registration always creates a standard member account.

A public user cannot choose owner, admin, manager or viewer.
User profile editing cannot change role or permissions.
Administrative user management must be a separate, restricted control-plane capability.
Admins may manage account status and abuse/security cases but do not automatically gain access to private financial records.
The current Users & Roles tab must not be visible to ordinary customers.
Any internal account-support access to customer data, if ever required, must be separately designed, limited, explicitly audited and preferably consent-based.
Remove ordinary impersonation as a shortcut for viewing a user's ledger.
Do not expose bulk user counts or audit details to all authenticated accounts.
7.6 Existing shared-workspace migration
This is a high-risk migration and must be planned before public registration is enabled.

Current code includes workspaceMode values shared and isolated, with several roles defaulting to shared and requests redirected to the primary owner's ledger. The target design removes that model.

Migration rules:

Take and verify a database backup before changing user ownership behavior.
Inventory users, roles, workspace modes, and counts of all user-owned records.
Identify records stored under the primary owner's ID and records stored under other user IDs.
Preserve the existing owner account's own historical data under that account.
Do not automatically copy shared-ledger records to every user who previously had shared access.
Do not guess record ownership from display names, notes, email addresses or transaction descriptions.
Produce a migration report for any legacy account whose prior shared access makes data ownership ambiguous.
Set all ordinary accounts to the new private-account model.
Disable the shared-ledger resolution path and remove shared workspace defaults from roles.
Re-run cross-user isolation tests after migration.
Keep a rollback path and migration audit log.
Existing users should initially retain the Advanced experience so that currently available features are not silently hidden. They can choose Basic later. This is a recommended grandfathering default, not a reason to grant any existing user access to another account's data.

7.7 Security test examples
For two test accounts, Alice and Bob:

Alice cannot list Bob's transactions.
Alice cannot fetch Bob's wallet by guessing its UUID.
Alice cannot update or delete Bob's budget by supplying its ID.
Alice cannot settle Bob's debt.
Alice cannot import a statement into Bob's wallet.
Alice cannot retrieve Bob's investment portfolio or Binance data.
Alice cannot export Bob's records.
Alice cannot read Bob's notifications or push subscriptions.
Alice cannot alter Bob's profile, tier or role.
Changing userId in a request body has no effect.
A relationship that points to another user's wallet is rejected even if the record ID is valid.
Signing out or revoking a session prevents subsequent use of the old refresh token.
These checks must exist as automated integration tests, not only as manual QA instructions.

