9.3 Welcome and privacy explanation
Explain in plain language:

TrueSpend helps users understand their money and financial commitments.
Each account has its own private financial data.
Other users cannot browse or share the account's financial records.
The user controls their experience tier and can change it later.
The application is a personal finance management tool, not a bank or a guarantee of investment performance.
9.4 Tier selection screen
Show two equally valid choices.

Basic — Everyday money, made clear

Track accounts, income, expenses and transfers.
Manage budgets, debts, subscriptions and savings goals.
See calendar events and useful summaries.
Keep the interface focused and easy to navigate.
Advanced — Your complete financial command center

Includes everything in Basic.
Adds investment tracking, simulator tools, deeper forecasts, advanced analytics, automation and richer financial workflows.
Both cards must say Free. Do not label Advanced as a trial or imply that payment is required.

9.5 Account setup
Offer Bank, Cash and Savings account templates.
Ask for an account name and currency.
Opening balance is optional.
Explain that an opening balance is a starting point, not income.
Never create a fake transaction to represent an opening balance if the ledger model supports a true opening-balance entry.
Do not connect to a bank automatically in this update.
9.6 Getting-started checklist
Show a dismissible checklist on the dashboard until the user completes or dismisses it.

Suggested items:

Confirm your email.
Choose your experience.
Create your first account.
Record your first transaction.
Set up a monthly budget.
Add an upcoming bill or subscription.
Create a savings goal.
Checklist completion must be based on persisted user actions, not a browser-only flag.

9.7 Onboarding state
Persist:

onboarding_version.
onboarding_started_at.
onboarding_completed_at.
onboarding_dismissed_at.
Optional step completion metadata.
Do not store unnecessary personal financial information in onboarding analytics. Keep the state versioned so the application can introduce a new optional guide later without forcing every existing user through registration again.

10. Templates and starter recommendations
Templates should help users start quickly without making assumptions about their financial life.

10.1 Basic templates
Simple Monthly Budget — categories for essentials, daily spending, savings and flexible expenses.
50/30/20 Starter Budget — an optional educational starting point, not a mandatory rule.
Payday-Based Budget — a period based on the user's configured payday rather than assuming a calendar month.
Essential Accounts Setup — Bank and Cash accounts, with an optional Savings account.
First Savings Goal — a goal with target amount and optional deadline.
Recurring Bills Checklist — a starter checklist for rent, utilities, phone, internet and subscriptions.
10.2 Advanced templates
Advanced Envelope Budget — category envelopes, rollover and planned allocations.
Financial Safety Plan — emergency buffer and cash runway targets.
Debt Repayment Planner — multiple debts, due dates and optional APR/installment information.
Long-Term Savings Plan — goals and planned contribution cadence.
Investment Tracking Setup — brokerage/investment accounts and portfolio tracking, without making investment recommendations.
Life Event / Trip Budget — dedicated financial context with start/end dates and a budget.
Advanced Payday Allocation — custom allocation rules and payday-cycle planning.
10.3 Template behavior
Templates are optional and can be previewed before applying.
Applying a template creates editable user-owned records.
Applying the same template twice must not silently duplicate budgets or accounts.
Templates must not contain real people's financial data.
Templates should have a version and stable identifier.
Users can edit or delete records created from a template.
Applying a template must be transactional where multiple records are created.
Do not automatically apply a template based on inferred salary, debt or other sensitive data.
Recommendations should explain why a template may be useful and let the user decide.
11. Account lifecycle and data rights
11.1 Account states
Recommended states:

pending_verification
active
suspended
deletion_pending
deleted or a documented tombstone/anonymized state
Retain a narrowly scoped legacy state only while old records are migrated. Do not use legacy_hidden as a permanent public account state.

11.2 Data export
Provide a self-service export for the authenticated account.

Minimum scope:

Profile and preferences.
Accounts/wallets.
Transactions and splits.
Budgets.
Debts and settlements.
Goals and contributions.
Payrolls.
Subscriptions and commitments.
Contexts.
Relevant investment records for Advanced users.
A manifest describing export format and timestamps.
Export must be scoped to the authenticated user and must not include authentication secrets, password hashes, other users' data, or internal application secrets.

11.3 Account deletion
Require re-authentication or a recent verified session.
Explain which data will be deleted and what may remain temporarily in backups or security logs.
Offer an explicit confirmation step.
Revoke sessions and integration credentials immediately.
Delete or anonymize user-owned data according to the published retention policy.
Ensure foreign-key cascades cannot affect another user's records.
Record a minimal security/audit event without retaining unnecessary personal financial information.
Document backup expiration and restore behavior so a deleted account is not unexpectedly recreated from an old backup.
A deletion grace period may be used, but the duration and legal requirements must be confirmed before launch. Do not claim deletion is instantaneous if backups retain data for a defined period.

