14. API authorization model
Every protected endpoint should pass three distinct checks:

Authentication: Is the caller a valid, active, verified account?
Ownership/role authorization: Does the authenticated user own this resource, and does their role permit the requested operation?
Experience entitlement: Is this capability available in the user's selected tier?
These checks must be implemented centrally where possible and reinforced in service/repository methods.

Example policy:

requireAuth
requireVerifiedAccount
requireFeature('investments')
requireResourceOwnership
The actual middleware composition may differ, but no route should omit the required checks.

A tier must never grant additional access to another user's data. An administrator role must never bypass ownership checks for ordinary financial records.

15. Email delivery and public deployment readiness
The registration lifecycle depends on reliable email delivery.

Select a transactional email provider after reviewing price, sender-domain requirements, regional deliverability and operational needs.
Configure a verified sender domain.
Configure SPF, DKIM and DMARC.
Use templates for verification, password reset, password changed, email changed, new sign-in and security notifications.
Do not include passwords or raw long-lived credentials in email.
Keep email sending asynchronous with retry/monitoring.
Do not mark an email as verified merely because an email-send operation succeeded.
No email vendor is selected by this specification.

Production requirements:

HTTPS/TLS enforced.
Strict CORS allowlist for the web application and explicitly supported Capacitor origins.
Secure cookie configuration.
Trusted proxy configuration if deployed behind a reverse proxy.
Rate limiting and request logging without secrets.
Authentication secret validation at startup.
Database backups and tested restore procedure.
Error monitoring and authentication/security alerts.
Privacy policy and terms of service published before public launch.
No production default owner password or public seed endpoint.
Separate development/staging/production environments.
16. Privacy, transparency and financial safety
Before public launch, provide:

Privacy policy explaining what is stored and why.
Terms of service.
Clear account and data deletion process.
Data export process.
Explanation of optional integrations and their permissions.
Explanation that the user is responsible for the accuracy of manually entered records.
Clear distinction between financial tracking/education and regulated financial advice.
No promise of investment returns or guaranteed forecast accuracy.
User-controlled notification preferences.
No selling or sharing of financial records as a product feature.
Avoid using sensitive financial data for marketing, referrals or tier recommendations without explicit consent.

17. Testing and acceptance criteria
17.1 Registration and authentication
A new visitor can register without an invitation.
A valid optional invitation is accepted and attributed.
Registration with an invalid invitation can still proceed without the code.
Duplicate email is rejected safely, including concurrent registration attempts.
New accounts cannot choose privileged roles or another user's data scope.
Unverified accounts cannot use financial APIs.
Verification tokens expire and are single-use.
Forgot-password responses do not reveal whether an account exists.
Reset tokens expire and are single-use.
Resetting a password revokes previous sessions.
Logout revokes the current refresh session.
Logout-all revokes every active session.
Refresh-token rotation and reuse detection work.
Missing production secrets stop startup.
Default administrator credentials cannot authenticate in production.
Existing scrypt hashes can still be validated and upgraded.
17.2 User isolation
Automated tests prove cross-user read/write/delete/export denial.
Tampering with request userId never changes data ownership.
Every user-owned API route has an ownership test.
Cross-user foreign-key relationships are rejected.
Account deletion affects only the account being deleted.
Public users cannot list customer accounts.
Admin/support operations cannot silently impersonate a customer or read their ledger.
Background jobs and exports remain user-scoped.
17.3 Basic and Advanced
Basic and Advanced both work without any payment configuration.
Basic dashboard and core financial workflows are useful and complete.
Simulator/Calculators and Investments are unavailable in Basic through both UI and API.
Advanced exposes the complete approved feature set.
Tier switching works without logging out.
Downgrading preserves all data.
Upgrading immediately restores access to existing Advanced data.
Tier restrictions are distinct from role restrictions.
Deep links and direct API requests respect entitlements.
17.4 Onboarding
New users see onboarding after verification.
Users can skip optional financial setup.
Users can choose either tier.
Users can apply a template once without accidental duplicates.
Checklist progress persists across devices/sessions.
Existing users are not forced through new registration.
Onboarding can be dismissed and revisited from Settings/help.
17.5 Operational and quality checks
Database migration is tested against a production-like backup.
Migration can be rolled back or recovered.
Email delivery failure can be retried.
Authentication endpoints are rate-limited.
CORS and cookies are tested on browser and Android Capacitor.
TypeScript/build checks pass.
Existing financial calculation and ledger tests remain green.
New security tests run in CI.
No secrets or private data appear in application logs.
18. Recommended delivery phases
Phase 0 — Full security and data-ownership audit
Inventory every authenticated route and every user-owned table.
Trace all req.dbUser, workspaceMode, owner bootstrap and impersonation behavior.
Identify every repository query that trusts a client-provided user ID.
Review authentication and sensitive integration secrets.
Review CORS, cookies, environment configuration and deployment assumptions.
Produce a legacy data ownership report.
Do not enable public registration yet.
Exit condition: A complete route/table ownership map and a safe migration plan exist.

Phase 1 — Private-account foundation
Remove shared-workspace resolution.
Make authenticated user ID the only customer-data owner.
Remove public-account access to the owner's ledger.
Add ownership-scoped repository/service methods.
Fix user-management route authorization.
Add database constraints/indexes for account identity and tenant relationships.
Migrate legacy accounts without guessing the owner of ambiguous records.
Add cross-user isolation integration tests.
Exit condition: All existing accounts operate on separate data, and cross-user tests pass.

Phase 2 — Authentication hardening
Remove hardcoded fallback credentials and fallback JWT secret.
Implement password policy and legacy hash upgrade.
Implement secure sessions, refresh rotation and revocation.
Add registration and verification.
Add forgot/reset password.
Add email-change and password-change flows.
Add rate limiting and strict CORS/origin validation.
Support browser and Capacitor Android session behavior.
Add authentication security events.
Exit condition: Account lifecycle works without Firebase Authentication and passes security tests.

Phase 3 — Profile and account controls
Build profile/settings sections.
Add active session management.
Add export and account deletion workflows.
Protect integration credentials.
Add privacy and terms pages.
Exit condition: Users can manage their account and data without administrator intervention.

Phase 4 — Tier and entitlement system
Add experience_tier.
Implement centralized feature entitlement mapping.
Add backend guards.
Add tier-aware navigation and UI.
Implement Basic simplifications and Advanced full experience.
Add tier-switching flow and data-preservation tests.
Exit condition: Both free experiences work and no tier restriction relies only on frontend hiding.

Phase 5 — Onboarding and templates
Add welcome and tier-selection screens.
Add optional profile/preferences setup.
Add wallet/account setup.
Add Basic and Advanced starter templates.
Add first-use checklist and persisted progress.
Add contextual help and demo/sample-data option if approved.
Exit condition: A new user can register, configure the essentials and understand how to start using TrueSpend.

Phase 6 — Public-launch readiness
Verify email deliverability and domain authentication.
Finalize privacy policy and terms.
Verify production secrets and environment settings.
Test browser and Android deployments.
Run migration rehearsal and restore rehearsal.
Run security, load and end-to-end regression suites.
Enable public registration only after all release gates pass.
Exit condition: A new external user can register and use a private Basic or Advanced account safely.

19. Out of scope
This update does not include:

Paid subscriptions or payment processing.
Subscription billing, invoices, checkout pages or payment-provider integration.
Shared workspaces or shared ledgers.
Joint/family accounts.
Firebase Authentication migration.
Bank account aggregation or open-banking connections.
Mandatory two-factor authentication on day one (the architecture should support it).
A new investment recommendation engine.
Rebuilding the financial ledger or replacing the existing accounting model.
These can be considered separately without weakening the account and isolation foundations.

20. Risks and mitigations
Risk	Impact	Mitigation
Legacy shared-ledger data is assigned to the wrong person	Critical privacy breach or data loss	Inventory and classify before migration; never auto-copy ambiguous records
A route misses user ownership checks	Cross-account data exposure	Route/table inventory, centralized auth context and automated negative tests
Frontend-only tier gating	Advanced APIs remain accessible to Basic	Enforce entitlements on backend routes/services
Token compromise	Account takeover	Short-lived access tokens, rotating refresh sessions, secure storage and revocation
Email deliverability failure	Users cannot verify or recover accounts	Verified sender domain, outbox/retry and resend flows
Tier logic becomes duplicated	Inconsistent UI/API behavior	Central entitlement mapping and shared feature keys
Basic becomes too limited	Poor adoption	Preserve core money-management workflows and test with non-developer users
Account deletion conflicts with backups	Deleted data reappears after restore	Document retention, backup expiry and restore-time deletion tombstones
Admin access becomes an unintended data-access path	Loss of user trust	Separate control plane from private financial data; audited break-glass access only
21. Open implementation decisions
These items can be decided during the detailed implementation design. They do not change the product decisions already confirmed.

Email provider: Select a transactional email provider and configure sender-domain authentication.
Username: Decide whether username remains a required public login identifier or becomes optional with email as the canonical login.
Native session storage: Select and test secure token storage for Capacitor Android.
Password hashing dependency: Confirm whether Argon2id is supported by the deployment/build environment; otherwise use asynchronous scrypt.
Two-factor timing: Decide whether authenticator-app TOTP is included in the initial release or the immediate security follow-up.
Account deletion retention: Confirm grace period, backup retention and legal requirements before making public promises.
Language: Confirm launch languages and default locale. The onboarding architecture should support localization from the start.
Existing account migration: Review the generated user/record ownership report before changing any legacy shared-mode account.
22. Final recommendation
Do not begin with the registration UI. Begin with the private-account security foundation.

The most dangerous failure would be to add a polished public signup page while the middleware still redirects certain accounts to the developer's ledger or while one API route trusts a caller-supplied user ID.

Recommended implementation order:

Audit and remove shared-data behavior.
Prove account isolation with automated tests.
Harden the existing custom authentication and add the full account lifecycle.
Add the Basic/Advanced entitlement system.
Build onboarding and starter templates.
Complete privacy, data-rights and operational launch requirements.
Public registration is a release gate, not an early development toggle.

